WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.700
- [Instructor] We're already familiar

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:04.500
with the idea of a polynomial

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.650
and we've spent some
time adding polynomials,

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:09.140
subtracting polynomials,

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:10.580
and multiplying polynomials,

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:12.070
and factoring polynomials.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:13.790
And what we're going to
think about in this video

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:15.870
and really start to
think about in this video

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:20.020
is the idea of polynomial division.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.140
So, for example.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.110
If I had the polynomial,

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.290
and this would be a quadratic polynomial,

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:31.270
let's say x squared plus three x

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:36.830
plus two, and I wanted to divide it

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:39.470
by x plus one.

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:40.610
Pause this video and think about

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:42.120
what would that be?

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.950
What would I have to
multiply x plus one by

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.653
to get x squared plus three x plus two?

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:50.140
Well, one way to approach it is

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:51.760
we could try to factor x squared

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:52.880
plus three x plus two

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.970
and we've done that
multiple times in our lives.

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:58.200
We think about well, what
two numbers add up to three

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.480
and if I were to multiply them I get two.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:01.730
And the one that might jump,

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:03.390
or the ones that might jump out at you

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:04.710
are two and one.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:06.530
And so we could express x squared

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:10.260
plus three x plus two as x plus two

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.590
times x plus one, and then all of that

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:17.600
is going to be over x plus one.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.600
And so, if you were to take x plus two

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.810
times x plus one, and then divide that

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:24.580
by x plus one, what is that going to be?

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:26.350
Well, you're just going to be left with

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:27.910
an x plus two.

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:29.590
This is going, you don't
have to put parentheses,

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:31.600
this is going to be an x plus two.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.770
And if we want to be really
mathematically precise

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:35.410
we would say hey, this would be true

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:38.140
as long as x does not equal,

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.990
x does not equal negative one,

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:42.640
because if x equals negative one

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.960
in this expression or this expression

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:46.560
we're gonna be dividing by zero.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.030
And we know that leads to all sorts

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:49.900
of mathematical problems.

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:52.160
But as we see, for any other x

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.920
as long as we're not
dividing by zero here,

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.280
this expression is going to be

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:57.590
the same thing as x plus two,

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.340
and that's because x plus two

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.990
times x plus one is equal to what we have

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:04.050
in this numerator here.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:08.000
Now, as we go deeper
into polynomial division,

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.860
we're going to approach things

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.210
that aren't as easy to do

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:12.720
just purely through factoring.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:14.410
And that's where we're
gonna have a technique

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:17.570
called polynomial long division.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.250
Polynomial long division,

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:23.470
sometimes known as
algebraic long division.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:25.790
And if it sounds familiar,

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:27.750
because you first learned
about long division

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:28.680
in fourth or fifth grade,

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.070
it's because it's a very similar process

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:34.260
where you would take your x plus one

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.020
and you would try to divide it

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:41.020
into your x squared plus three x plus two.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:42.950
And you do.

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:45.180
Something very, and I'm
gonna do a very quick example

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:46.360
right over here, but we're gonna do

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:47.810
much more detailed examples

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:49.390
in future videos, but you look

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:50.570
at the highest degree terms.

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:52.280
You say okay, I have a first degree term,

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:53.710
I have a second degree term here.

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:56.100
How many times is x going to x squared?

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.170
Well, it goes x times.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.850
So you put the x in
the first degree column

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:03.620
and then you multiply
your x times x plus one.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:05.660
X times x is x squared.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:07.220
X times one is x.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:10.320
And then you subtract this from that.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.000
So you might already start to see

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.980
some parallels with the long division

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.610
that you first learned
in school many years ago.

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:18.660
So when you do that, these cancel out,

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:20.080
three x minus x.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:22.280
We are left with a two x.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:23.900
And then you bring down that two.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:25.207
So two x plus two.

00:03:25.207 --> 00:03:27.130
And you say how many times does x

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:28.550
go into two x?

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:30.350
Well, it goes two times.

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:32.000
So you have a plus two here.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.180
Two times x plus one.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:35.700
Two times x is two x.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.440
Two times one is two.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.950
You can subtract these and then

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:42.490
you are going to be left with nothing.

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:43.800
Two minus two is zero.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.300
Two x minus two x is zero.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:48.610
So in this situation it divided

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:51.570
cleanly into it and we got x plus two,

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:54.570
which is exactly what we had over there.

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:55.910
Now, an interesting scenario

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:57.430
that we are also going to approach

00:03:57.430 --> 00:03:58.890
in the next few videos,

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.350
is what if things don't divide cleanly?

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:03.700
For example, if I were to add one

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:06.170
to x squared plus three x plus two,

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:11.170
I would get x squared
plus three x plus three.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:12.770
And if I were to try to divide that

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:15.510
by x plus one, well, it's not going

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:16.870
to divide cleanly anymore.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:18.870
You could it either approach.

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:20.370
One way to think about it, if we know

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:22.450
we can factor x squared
plus three x plus two

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:25.330
is say hey, this is the same thing as

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:30.330
x squared plus three x plus two plus one,

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:34.320
and then all of that's
going to be over x plus one.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:35.590
And then you could say hey,

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:36.810
this is the same thing

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:41.020
as x squared plus three x plus two

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.350
over x plus one,

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:44.820
over x plus one,

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:47.050
plus one over x plus one.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:51.040
Plus one over x plus one.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:52.530
And we already figured out

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:54.640
that this expression on the left,

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:57.440
as long as x does not equal negative one,

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:00.080
this is going to be equal to x plus two.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:02.000
So this is going to be
equal to x plus two,

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.250
but then we have that one

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:05.770
that we were able to
divide x plus one into,

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:08.540
so we're just left with
the one over x plus one.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:10.610
And we'll study that in a lot more detail

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:11.443
in other videos.

00:05:11.443 --> 00:05:12.800
What does this remainder mean

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:13.950
and how do we calculate it

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:15.880
if we can't factor part of what we have

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:16.820
in the numerator?

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:19.160
And as we do our polynomial long division

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:21.150
we'll see that the remainder will show up

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:24.030
at the end when we are done dividing.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.153
We'll see those examples in future videos.

